dr. Don limbs job is emergency medicine  she's on the front line treating  patients with kovat 19 but there's  another layer to what she does after a  shift or on her days off she picks up  her camera and turns it on her  colleagues capturing what few of us see  the care the precision and the  unpredictability of what it takes to  save lives dr. Lim wants to create a  permanent record of how this pandemic  plays out she's shared her photographs  with us my name is dr. Don Lim I'm an  emergency physician at the University  Health Network which is Toronto General  Hospital and Toronto Western Hospital  that is porn half one was five and my  daughter is 16 months now so every time  I say goodbye I close the door and my  son's always waiting for me with my  daughter and my husband the bangs on the  window and he says goodbye it makes a  little heart with his index finger and  his thumb and he can't he tries to shout  I love you mom and goodbye and but  that's what he means when he says that  to make sure that I'm brave and as soon  as I get back everything comes off and  goes straight into the wash and I don't  I don't hug my kids I don't say even  hello to them until I've had a really  long shower when the fear of the  pandemic and the unknown started that's  when we started actually talking to our  colleagues who had gone through SARS and  what was that like and one of them told  me that it was very hard for her team  because afterwards when you know the  last patient was cured and they lifted  the restrictions it wasn't something  that they talked about and so I felt  that I felt like this story couldn't be  forgotten the same way that stars was  forgotten because we wanted to make sure  that each person with Cova door  suspected kovat had the minimal exposure  to team members the hospital actually  created a team called the ko Delta team  which is an intubation team staffed by  and anesthesia assistant a nurse and a  staff Aneesa test they're checking their  equipment they're making sure they have  all their medications and they're making  sure that they're completely covered and  safe for the intubation I felt at the  very beginning I thought maybe it was a  crazy idea to try to document the  pandemic because I thought maybe people  wouldn't be receptive when I brought up  this idea that people were very  supportive of it and they knew that if  we didn't talk about it then the story  would be forgotten just like SARS the  patient is about to be intubated it's  very challenging so they decompensate  very quickly so you pretty much have one  shot to get the intubation before they  go get into trouble and the person who's  doing the intubation really wants to  minimize any type of procedure that will  increase aerosolization so it's a first  shot one shot do it right  usually we are used to dealing with  patients who are very sick that's not  new but patients with kovat they they  look well until they don't look well and  that's been very scary visitors aren't  allowed and so when we do have patients  who died not being able to close that  circle and have the family nearby has  been really tough for the team so she's  got the baby monitor in her hand and  she's looking very carefully to see what  the team needs so if you imagine the  face shields and the face mask it's very  hard to hear people and you've also got  the wall of clothes only a few can stay  inside and everyone's on the outside and  everyone's waiting to see what's going  to happen and waiting to make sure that  the team inside has everything that they  need he and his team are responsible for  making sure that the room is  meticulously cleaned after each patient  and you see that he's he's doing a lot  of work to do to keep a keep to keep the  whole team safe we really appreciate  them a lot of the pictures of people's  eyes are so focused and you see such a  concentration  but I do see a strain on people people's  faces and I do see the uncertainty in  their eyes because we're there to help  them and at the same time we feel very  vulnerable ourselves and that's very  hard to talk about I think it's very  common for healthcare providers to not  feel that they necessarily can ask for  help or if they do need help there's a  stigma associated with that sometimes  and this is the the pause that our teams  do at the time of death to pay respects  to the patient that pre kovat it it was  quite common to have the family members  nearby but this patient had only the  medical team to say goodbye so you know  looking at this I think this was a very  special moment for this team as well  because they they knew that what they  had they tried to do everything they  could and still they lost the patient so  it's the time of death so a very moving  photo for the team as well  storytelling for me is the most powerful  tool that we have for connection and  healing but I hope that by doing this  photo essay people remember that feeling  that they had when we came together as a  team that's what I hope that they take  away from it because I think that's the  power of art it gives us permission to  pause and reflect  [Music]
 

